{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Joseph could not refrain himself before all those who stood upon him.</b> He could not bear that the Egyptians be standing upon him and hearing while his brothers were put to shame when he made himself known to them — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 45:1). And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 45:1), \"לְהִתְאַפֵּק\" — to bear. \"לְכָל הַנִּצָּבִים עָלָיו\" — its meaning is: until all those standing upon him would go out, and he was forced to call to have them removed. But Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 45:1) translated \"לְאִתְחַסָּנָא\" — to strengthen himself, and so too \"וָאֶתְאַפַּק וָאַעֲלֶה הָעֹלָה\" (1 Samuel 13:12), and so every form of הִתְאַפְּקוּת in every place means strengthening. And what is correct in my eyes is that there were there, from the house of Pharaoh and from the Egyptians, many men entreating his face to pardon Benjamin, for their compassion was stirred by the supplications of Judah, and Joseph could not strengthen himself against them all, and he called to his servants: \"Remove every foreign man from upon me,\" for I will speak with them, and they went out from upon him. And when they went out from upon him, he gave his voice in weeping, and the Egyptians and the men of Pharaoh's house who had been removed from upon him heard, for they were still in the outer court. And it is possible that the meaning of \"הַנִּצָּבִים עָלָיו\" is: his attendants who stand before him, like \"הַנַּעַר הַנִּצָּב עַל הַקּוֹצְרִים\" (Ruth 2:6), \"שָׂרֵי הַנִּצָּבִים\" (1 Kings 5:30), \"לְהִתְיַצֵּב עַל יְהוָה\" (Job 1:6). And the meaning of \"וַיִּקְרָא\" is that he raised his voice in anger and said to his attendants: \"Remove every man from upon me except these men.\" And the reason for the removal — that he removed them from there — is so that they would not hear when he mentioned to them the recognition, for it would be a stumbling block to them even with him, for Pharaoh's servants and the Egyptians would say about them: \"These are men of treachery; they shall not dwell in our land nor walk in our palaces; they betrayed their brother and also their father they betrayed — what would they do to the king and his people?\" And also in Joseph they would no longer trust."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:45:27.628572454Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}